00:35:33,617 --> 00:35:34,787
He is very satisfied.

797
00:35:35,857 --> 00:35:37,157
- It's pretty. - Okay.

798
00:35:37,157 --> 00:35:38,257
It's nice.

799
00:35:38,687 --> 00:35:42,297
All the aspects of the house go well together.

800
00:35:43,727 --> 00:35:47,967
(DAWN's steps are light from excitement.)

801
00:35:53,507 --> 00:35:54,507
(Picking up)

802
00:35:54,507 --> 00:35:56,277
What? I bet he made something.

803
00:35:56,277 --> 00:35:57,477
- What's that? - What? What's that?

804
00:35:57,477 --> 00:35:59,107
- Wire fence? - Wire?

805
00:35:59,107 --> 00:36:01,307
(DAWN brings something out.)

806
00:36:01,307 --> 00:36:02,577
Isn't it a bamboo frame?

807
00:36:02,777 --> 00:36:05,747
- That's why he brought bamboo. - A bamboo frame?

808
00:36:05,747 --> 00:36:07,317
(Shaking)

809
00:36:08,047 --> 00:36:11,827
(He sits on the sofa with the mystery item.)

810
00:36:12,957 --> 00:36:16,857
(Let's take a look.)

811
00:36:17,997 --> 00:36:19,467
It's rattan.

812
00:36:19,467 --> 00:36:20,667
- Rattan? - I see.

813
00:36:20,667 --> 00:36:22,397
It's often used for craft.

814
00:36:22,397 --> 00:36:25,967
I'll use it to make a light.

815
00:36:26,567 --> 00:36:27,867
Will you make it?

816
00:36:27,867 --> 00:36:31,077
I like lights that spread out gently.

817
00:36:31,807 --> 00:36:35,747
I didn't have the money to buy expensive lights,

818
00:36:36,417 --> 00:36:37,577
so I decided to make them.

819
00:36:38,177 --> 00:36:39,947
I've been making them ever since.

820
00:36:40,847 --> 00:36:42,017
How cool.

821
00:36:42,957 --> 00:36:44,887
- Did you make that hanging light? - Yes.

822
00:36:45,787 --> 00:36:46,957
That was my first work.

823
00:36:47,987 --> 00:36:49,457
My very first work.

824
00:36:49,457 --> 00:36:50,657
You have a talent.

825
00:36:53,027 --> 00:36:56,697
I use rattan to make a frame.

826
00:36:58,667 --> 00:36:59,907
(After making the frame, he secures it with a glue gun.)

827
00:36:59,907 --> 00:37:02,077
- Do you use a glue gun? - It can't be easy.

828
00:37:02,077 --> 00:37:03,177
He has a glue gun.

829
00:37:03,177 --> 00:37:04,177
(After making the frame, he secures it with a glue gun.)

830
00:37:05,007 --> 00:37:08,177
(Focused)

831
00:37:09,717 --> 00:37:15,417
(The glue secures the frame as it hardens.)

832
00:37:18,527 --> 00:37:22,297
(He adds one rattan stick at a time...)

833
00:37:23,427 --> 00:37:27,697
(until the frame is done.)

834
00:37:29,367 --> 00:37:30,497
He is absorbed in his work.

835
00:37:30,737 --> 00:37:31,967
You make your own designs...

836
00:37:31,967 --> 00:37:33,367
- instead of copying them, right? - Of course.

837
00:37:33,367 --> 00:37:36,507
- That's right. I go with the flow. - That's the best method.

838
00:37:38,377 --> 00:37:41,447
(He puts one piece...)

839
00:37:41,447 --> 00:37:44,977
(after another...)

840
00:37:44,977 --> 00:37:47,617
(with concentration.)

841
00:37:47,617 --> 00:37:49,457
He makes it like a master.

842
00:37:49,457 --> 00:37:52,287
- Like an intangible cultural asset. - Seriously.

843
00:37:52,287 --> 00:37:53,827
(He is cool.)

844
00:37:54,757 --> 00:38:00,167
(The light frame takes shape.)

845
00:38:11,207 --> 00:38:16,717
(He cuts the pieces that are sticking out.)

846
00:38:20,287 --> 00:38:21,387
Okay.

847
00:38:21,387 --> 00:38:24,817
(The light frame is ready.)

848
00:38:26,887 --> 00:38:29,227
(Mixing)

849
00:38:30,657 --> 00:38:33,327
- I use wood glue. - Wood glue.

850
00:38:33,327 --> 00:38:35,237
I dilute it with water for my use.

851
00:38:37,137 --> 00:38:40,267
You need to dilute it with water for it to dry quickly.

852
00:38:40,507 --> 00:38:41,607
That's right.

853
00:38:42,337 --> 00:38:44,807
(Tearing)

854
00:38:46,807 --> 00:38:48,977
Afterward, I stick...

855
00:38:49,247 --> 00:38:50,917
Korean paper on the frame.

856
00:38:52,417 --> 00:38:54,987
When there are multiple layers of paper,

857
00:38:55,157 --> 00:38:57,817
the light spreads out as if it's a diffuser...

858
00:38:58,157 --> 00:39:00,887
and slowly glows up the room.

859
00:39:02,527 --> 00:39:03,857
I developed it myself.

860
00:39:05,197 --> 00:39:08,497
You've done a lot of observing.

861
00:39:08,497 --> 00:39:10,597
- I've done a lot of research. - Right.

862
00:39:10,597 --> 00:39:11,767
That's really hard to do.

863
00:39:11,767 --> 00:39:12,937
(His past efforts can be seen through his hands.)

864
00:39:15,037 --> 00:39:19,177
(He places each piece carefully.)

865
00:39:20,807 --> 00:39:27,787
(Meanwhile, it became dark outside.)

866
00:39:29,187 --> 00:39:33,257
(He's focused on it without realizing time is flying by.)

867
00:39:38,667 --> 00:39:41,227
This makes me become very focused.

868
00:39:42,297 --> 00:39:44,067
It fills me with all kinds of thoughts.

869
00:39:45,467 --> 00:39:47,367
Rattan actually breaks easily...

870
00:39:47,367 --> 00:39:49,837
before becoming wet with water.

871
00:39:50,437 --> 00:39:52,607
It becomes flexible when it gets wet.

872
00:39:52,847 --> 00:39:54,347
Even when seeing things like that,

873
00:39:54,877 --> 00:39:57,617
it makes me think that a person can break...

874
00:39:58,047 --> 00:39:59,517
if they become too strong.

875
00:39:59,517 --> 00:40:00,687
He even thinks philosophically.

876
00:40:01,087 --> 00:40:03,117
- Becoming flexible... - Right.

877
00:40:03,117 --> 00:40:04,317
would prevent me from breaking...

878
00:40:05,087 --> 00:40:06,327
and living a harder life.

879
00:40:08,557 --> 00:40:11,127
The glue from the glue gun...

880
00:40:11,297 --> 00:40:12,827
is really hot when I touch it from time to time.

881
00:40:13,027 --> 00:40:15,437
When it hardens to a certain point,

882
00:40:15,697 --> 00:40:18,337
I can touch it and change its form.

883
00:40:19,607 --> 00:40:22,477
"When people become hot with emotion,"

884
00:40:23,837 --> 00:40:25,577
"they must not be touched."

885
00:40:26,077 --> 00:40:27,247
Right.

886
00:40:27,407 --> 00:40:30,177
- "When the two cool down..." - I like that.

887
00:40:30,177 --> 00:40:32,147
"and soothe each other,"

888
00:40:32,447 --> 00:40:34,987
"that is when they can form a shape together."

889
00:40:34,987 --> 00:40:36,387
I get these kinds of thoughts.

890
00:40:37,917 --> 00:40:40,457
While fiddling with my hands, I get lost in thought.

891
00:40:41,227 --> 00:40:43,557
I usually sit there for around 6 to 7 hours.

892
00:40:43,557 --> 00:40:44,697
(While quietly getting lost in thought as he uses his hands,)

893
00:40:45,697 --> 00:40:49,067
(he uses this time to solely focus on himself.)

894
00:40:56,637 --> 00:41:00,977
(He finishes up by drying the glue on the Korean paper.)

895
00:41:05,987 --> 00:41:10,017
(The end of the work can be seen.)

896
00:41:10,657 --> 00:41:12,627
(Sleeping)

897
00:41:12,857 --> 00:41:14,587
Whenever I make something,

898
00:41:14,587 --> 00:41:17,597
I give it to the people around me as a gift.

899
00:41:17,627 --> 00:41:19,027
Are you planning to give us gifts as well?

900
00:41:19,097 --> 00:41:21,427
I give it as a gift if someone moves into a new place.

901
00:41:21,427 --> 00:41:23,467
- I'm moving soon. - I'll move.

902
00:41:24,597 --> 00:41:26,707
Right. You can start looking for a new place.

903
00:41:27,307 --> 00:41:28,437
I called dibs.

904
00:41:31,507 --> 00:41:34,377
(DAWN checks the bamboo he chopped in the afternoon.)

905
00:41:35,107 --> 00:41:37,517
(This one should do.)

906
00:41:38,217 --> 00:41:39,947
- I'm really curious about... - What is that for?

907
00:41:39,947 --> 00:41:41,117
- the bamboo he brought. - Right.

908
00:41:41,117 --> 00:41:42,687
I don't have a clue what it's for.

909
00:41:43,257 --> 00:41:46,027
(He picks two stalks of bamboo that are of different thickness.)

910
00:41:48,897 --> 00:41:51,197
(Sawing)

911
00:41:55,767 --> 00:41:59,107
(He saws off part of it.)

912
00:42:04,407 --> 00:42:08,077
(Realizing)

913
00:42:08,077 --> 00:42:09,217
What is it?

914
00:42:11,117 --> 00:42:14,117
(DAWN makes a hole on the thick bamboo stalk.)

915
00:42:15,887 --> 00:42:17,787
- Gosh! - Here comes a masterpiece.

916
00:42:17,787 --> 00:42:19,787
(He makes the hole bigger with scissors.)

917
00:42:22,127 --> 00:42:27,067
(The thinner bamboo stalk is placed into the hole.)

918
00:42:27,097 --> 00:42:28,897
It's just like how nails aren't used...

919
00:42:28,897 --> 00:42:30,397
- when making traditional houses. - Right.

920
00:42:30,397 --> 00:42:31,937
Right.

921
00:42:33,467 --> 00:42:35,377
(He works on the wires next.)

922
00:42:35,377 --> 00:42:37,537
- He works like a professional. - Gosh.

923
00:42:37,837 --> 00:42:40,147
- He can do it all. - It's a light.

924
00:42:40,747 --> 00:42:46,387
(He puts together the bulb that will be used for the light.)

925
00:42:46,587 --> 00:42:50,217
As ready-made bulbs won't fit,

926
00:42:50,217 --> 00:42:52,027
- I make each part separately. - According to the size.

927
00:42:52,857 --> 00:42:54,927
(Pulling out)

928
00:42:55,827 --> 00:42:58,597
(The wire is placed through the connected bamboo stalks.)

929
00:42:59,197 --> 00:43:01,627
(Getting up)

930
00:43:05,467 --> 00:43:08,677
(The bulb that is connected is put into the frame.)

931
00:43:14,147 --> 00:43:17,817
(I'm done now.)

932
00:43:21,047 --> 00:43:22,257
Gosh, it looks nice.

933
00:43:27,657 --> 00:43:29,357
(Lighting up)

934
00:43:31,857 --> 00:43:36,367
(The light turns on after a long time spent.)

935
00:43:38,197 --> 00:43:40,037
(Impressed)

936
00:43:42,407 --> 00:43:44,777
It looks really nice as the bamboo stalk is tall.

937
00:43:44,777 --> 00:43:47,577
(The light has finally come to life.)

938
00:43:47,577 --> 00:43:48,917
- It looks nice. - It looks nice.

939
00:43:49,717 --> 00:43:51,147
Intangible Heritage 61.

940
00:43:52,617 --> 00:43:53,617
That's my dream.

941
00:43:54,487 --> 00:43:56,757
- He made that many of them? - Hold on. Intangible Heritage 61?

942
00:43:56,757 --> 00:43:58,617
So that's the 61st piece?

943
00:43:58,857 --> 00:44:00,687
I just went with 61 since my birthday is June 1.

944
00:44:01,287 --> 00:44:03,427
(I see!)

945
00:44:04,027 --> 00:44:07,597
I came up with a name for the first light that I made...

946
00:44:07,597 --> 00:44:08,897
on the first floor.

947
00:44:08,997 --> 00:44:11,397
It looked like a pupa.

948
00:44:11,697 --> 00:44:14,367
That was why I named it the pupa light.

949
00:44:15,107 --> 00:44:19,447
I really wanted to make another light after that.

950
00:44:20,177 --> 00:44:23,447
So I thought that I should make a light...

951
00:44:23,877 --> 00:44:26,087
expressing the butterfly coming out of the cocoon.

952
00:44:26,087 --> 00:44:28,317
- A butterfly... - There's a story to it.

953
00:44:28,387 --> 00:44:29,857
- coming out of the cocoon. - Gosh.

954
00:44:30,757 --> 00:44:33,987
So it's called the metamorphosis light.

955
00:44:34,927 --> 00:44:37,527
- It makes sense. - There's a story to it.

956
00:44:39,327 --> 00:44:41,797
(The metamorphosis light creator is satisfied with the result.)

957
00:44:41,797 --> 00:44:42,967
I feel so proud.

958
00:44:43,937 --> 00:44:45,297
I'm never going to do this again.

959
00:44:45,297 --> 00:44:48,407
(A twist)

960
00:44:48,567 --> 00:44:50,107
Even after declaring not to do it again,

961
00:44:50,377 --> 00:44:52,577
- You end up doing it again. - I end up doing it again.

962
00:44:52,577 --> 00:44:54,147
- It looks nice, though. - Right.

963
00:44:54,577 --> 00:44:57,317
(Gosh, that was exhausting.)

964
00:44:57,317 --> 00:44:58,417
I should eat now.

965
00:44:59,487 --> 00:45:00,517
I'm hungry.

966
00:45:01,787 --> 00:45:02,887
I should eat.

967
00:45:02,887 --> 00:45:05,927
(He heads to the kitchen while feeling exhausted.)

968
00:45:06,457 --> 00:45:08,357
(It's his first meal after having breakfast.)

969
00:45:08,357 --> 00:45:10,627
- It's his first meal. - Right.

970
00:45:10,757 --> 00:45:12,527
- He really doesn't eat. - Right.

971
00:45:13,297 --> 00:45:14,667
(Dinner is scorched rice?)

972
00:45:14,667 --> 00:45:16,037
- Gosh! - What?

973
00:45:16,897 --> 00:45:18,267
- Gosh. - What?

974
00:45:18,597 --> 00:45:19,607
Sungnyung?

975
00:45:19,607 --> 00:45:21,137
(He adds a generous amount of scorched rice to the pot.)

976
00:45:25,607 --> 00:45:27,577
I love scorched rice.

977
00:45:27,977 --> 00:45:31,477
(He opens the fridge.)

978
00:45:31,977 --> 00:45:33,787
(He heads back after taking something out.)

979
00:45:34,217 --> 00:45:36,657
(Eggs)

980
00:45:37,387 --> 00:45:39,687
- Eggs? Protein? - Protein?

981
00:45:40,527 --> 00:45:43,157
(Whisking)

982
00:45:43,897 --> 00:45:45,797
(He skilfully removes the chalaza as well.)

983
00:45:46,367 --> 00:45:48,367
(This should be enough.)

984
00:45:50,497 --> 00:45:51,997
He's steaming that. Gosh.

985
00:45:51,997 --> 00:45:54,567
(He's ready to make steamed eggs!)

986
00:45:56,507 --> 00:45:58,947
(He gets rid of the tuna oil with a strainer.)

987
00:45:58,947 --> 00:46:01,377
The oil is removed as it's bad for the body.

988
00:46:02,977 --> 00:46:05,217
(After heating up the instant rice,)

989
00:46:06,387 --> 00:46:08,517
(the strained tuna gets added.)

990
00:46:08,517 --> 00:46:10,487
- Rice balls? - He must be mixing it up.

991
00:46:11,887 --> 00:46:14,257
- Tuna mayo? - Gosh, pollack roe!

992
00:46:14,257 --> 00:46:16,227
- He knows how to eat! - Pollack roe mayo!

993
00:46:16,227 --> 00:46:17,457
(The tuna pollack roe mayo is ready.)

994
00:46:17,927 --> 00:46:19,327
(I can't resist it!)

995
00:46:19,327 --> 00:46:21,527
Right. That's how you eat at home.

996
00:46:21,527 --> 00:46:23,037
(Satisfied)

997
00:46:25,067 --> 00:46:28,407
(He gets a piece of gamtae next.)

998
00:46:33,747 --> 00:46:35,777
(He crushed the gamtae into a powder.)

999
00:46:36,417 --> 00:46:38,347
(After picking up a fistful of tuna pollack roe mayo...)

1000
00:46:38,447 --> 00:46:40,547
- I see. Rice balls. - It's rolled up?

1001
00:46:40,817 --> 00:46:42,587
It's rolled up after filling it up?

1002
00:46:42,587 --> 00:46:44,057
- Like this? - I see.

1003
00:46:45,517 --> 00:46:49,157
(After the powdered gamtae is added generously,)

1004
00:46:49,357 --> 00:46:53,327
(the tuna pollack roe mayo rice ball comes to life.)

1005
00:46:54,667 --> 00:46:59,707
(He makes several of them for him to eat.)

1006
00:46:59,707 --> 00:47:01,467
He came up with a simple meal.

1007
00:47:01,707 --> 00:47:03,477
- But he knows how to eat. - Right.

1008
00:47:03,477 --> 00:47:05,107
That looks good. Gosh, that's nice.

1009
00:47:06,377 --> 00:47:08,917
(The well-cooked steamed eggs get taken out.)

1010
00:47:10,017 --> 00:47:13,687
(A savory flavor is added with a little bit of sesame oil.)

1011
00:47:15,447 --> 00:47:17,417
I expected him to have a poor meal.

1012
00:47:17,417 --> 00:47:19,327
Right. Seeing how he has steamer,

1013
00:47:19,327 --> 00:47:20,787
- Right. - he knows how to make good meals.

1014
00:47:20,857 --> 00:47:22,157
I enjoy cooking.

1015
00:47:22,157 --> 00:47:23,757
I'm not very good, but I enjoy cooking.

1016
00:47:23,757 --> 00:47:25,157
You like making things in general.

1017
00:47:25,157 --> 00:47:26,227
Right.

1018
00:47:27,067 --> 00:47:29,397
That's hot.

1019
00:47:30,937 --> 00:47:34,137
The reason for making these dishes?

1020
00:47:34,307 --> 00:47:37,577
Actually, it was for one dish.

1021
00:47:38,977 --> 00:47:40,177
He must've bought something.

1022
00:47:40,177 --> 00:47:41,807
(He takes out a mysterious bag out from the fridge?)

1023
00:47:41,807 --> 00:47:43,147
- What could it be? - What could it be? Chicken feet?

1024
00:47:43,147 --> 00:47:44,177
- Raw fish? - Chicken feet or cartilage?

1025
00:47:44,177 --> 00:47:45,447
(He takes out a mysterious bag out from the fridge?)

1026
00:47:45,447 --> 00:47:46,487
Chicken feet?

1027
00:47:47,500 --> 00:47:48,700
He must've bought something.

1028
00:47:48,700 --> 00:47:50,200
(Excited)

1029
00:47:50,200 --> 00:47:51,600
- What could it be? - What could it be? Chicken feet?

1030
00:47:51,600 --> 00:47:52,740
- Raw fish? - Chicken feet or cartilage?

1031
00:47:53,010 --> 00:47:54,010
(What could that be?)

1032
00:47:54,010 --> 00:47:55,010
Chicken feet?

1033
00:47:59,750 --> 00:48:01,210
- Cold chicken feet. - Cold chicken feet.

1034
00:48:01,210 --> 00:48:02,580
- Cold chicken feet? - With rice balls,

1035
00:48:02,580 --> 00:48:03,850
- That calls for soju. - you must have chicken feet.

1036
00:48:05,550 --> 00:48:07,350
I always go to Paju for that.

1037
00:48:07,350 --> 00:48:09,020
You drive for an hour to have that?

1038
00:48:09,020 --> 00:48:10,690
- Yes. - It must be his favorite.

1039
00:48:10,690 --> 00:48:11,990
- That's understandable. - A round trip of two hours.

1040
00:48:11,990 --> 00:48:13,430
(He's fine with going on a round trip of two hours for it.)

1041
00:48:13,630 --> 00:48:16,560
- But the chicken feet are huge. - They're really big.

1042
00:48:16,600 --> 00:48:17,630
- They're really big. - They're really big.

1043
00:48:17,630 --> 00:48:18,930
They must be that big to be enjoyed properly.

1044
00:48:19,830 --> 00:48:20,930
(Amazed)

1045
00:48:20,930 --> 00:48:21,970
This is insane.

1046
00:48:27,370 --> 00:48:30,840
(He coats his stomach with the soft steamed eggs.)

1047
00:48:32,080 --> 00:48:33,080
(Satisfied)

1048
00:48:35,420 --> 00:48:36,480
(This time, he goes for the gamtae rice ball.)

1049
00:48:36,480 --> 00:48:38,850
It's the whole set including the scorched rice.

1050
00:48:39,020 --> 00:48:40,990
- Steamed eggs as well. - Right.

1051
00:48:43,027 --> 00:48:44,027
(Satisfied)

1052
00:48:44,060 --> 00:48:46,190
- He's really enjoying it. - It was really good.

1053
00:48:47,360 --> 00:48:48,460
- He eats well. - Chicken feet...

1054
00:48:48,460 --> 00:48:49,960
